In carbylamine reaction, aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic $\mathrm{KOH}$, form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances.

\begin{aligned}
& R-\mathrm{NH}_2+\mathrm{CHCl}_3+3 \mathrm{KOH}_{(\text {alc. })} \stackrel{\Delta}{\longrightarrow} \\
& R-\mathrm{NC}+3 \mathrm{KCl}+3 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \\
&
\end{aligned}

In an isocyanide, first an electrophile and then a nucleophile add at the carbon to form a species which usually undergoes further transformations.

\begin{array}{r}
R \stackrel{+}{\mathrm{N}} \equiv \overline{\mathrm{C}}+E^{+} \longrightarrow R \stackrel{+}{\mathrm{N}} \equiv \mathrm{CE} \stackrel{\mathrm{Nu}}{\longrightarrow} \mathrm{RN}=\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{Nu}) E \\
R_{+}^{\mathrm{N}} \equiv \overline{\mathrm{C}}+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \longrightarrow R \mathrm{~N}=\mathrm{CHOH} \longrightarrow R \mathrm{NHCHO}
\end{array}

